      "end_sec": 9.125,
      "duration_sec": 9.125,
      "narration": "Why does your phone charger get warm while fast-charging, or a ceiling fan slow down when voltage drops during summer in Bengaluru?",
      "visual": {
        "type": "title",
        "params": {
          "labels": [
            "Ohm's Law",
            "V = I × R",
            "Voltage, Current & Resistance"
          ],
          "steps": [
            "Everyday Electricity in India"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "start_sec": 9.125,
      "end_sec": 18.875,
      "duration_sec": 9.75,
      "narration": "Think of an overhead water tank on your house roof. Water height is Voltage, water flow through the pipe is Current, and a narrow tap is Resistance.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "compare",
        "params": {
          "labels": [
            "Roof Tank Height → Voltage (V)",
            "Pipe Water Flow → Current (I)",
            "Narrow Tap → Resistance (R)"
          ],
          "steps": [
            "Higher pressure pushes more flow",
            "Narrower pipe restricts flow"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "start_sec": 18.875,
      "end_sec": 30.5,
      "duration_sec": 11.625,
      "narration": "Georg Ohm discovered that Voltage equals Current multiplied by Resistance: V equals I times R. Doubling voltage doubles the current if resistance stays constant.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "equation",
        "params": {
          "labels": [
            "V = I × R",
            "V : Voltage (Volts)",
            "I : Current (Amperes)",
            "R : Resistance (Ohms Ω)"
          ],
          "steps": [
            "I = V / R",
            "R = V / I"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "start_sec": 30.5,
      "end_sec": 44.0,
      "duration_sec": 13.5,
      "narration": "Let's test a two hundred thirty volt Indian wall socket powering an iron box with forty-six ohms resistance. Two hundred thirty divided by forty-six gives five amperes of current.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "flow",
        "params": {
          "labels": [
            "Supply: V = 230 V",
            "Heater Coil: R = 46 Ω",
            "Formula: I = V / R",
            "Result: I = 5 A"
          ],
          "steps": [
            "230 V ÷ 46 Ω = 5 Amperes"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "start_sec": 44.0,
      "end_sec": 53.9,
      "duration_sec": 9.9,
      "narration": "To recap: Voltage pushes electrons, Resistance opposes them, and Current is the resulting flow governed by V equals I times R.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "bullets",
        "params": {
          "labels": [
            "Key Takeaways: Ohm's Law"
          ],
          "steps": [
            "Voltage (V): Electrical pressure in Volts",
            "Resistance (R): Opposition to flow in Ohms (Ω)",
            "Current (I = V / R): Flow rate in Amperes"
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
